    "lawCode": "Amd §4, Chap 522 of 2000",
    "lawSection": "State Finance",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5653                Revised 05/26/15\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 522 of the laws of 2000, amending the state\nfinance law and the general business law relating to establishing the\nunderground facilities safety training account, in relation to\nextending provisions of law relating to the underground facilities\nsafety training account\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo extend provisions of law relating to the underground facilities\nsafety training account from October 1, 2015 until October 1, 2020\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends section 4 of Chapter 522 of the laws of 2000, as\namended by Chapter 707 of the laws of 2005 and Chapter 372 of the laws\nof 2010, to extend provisions of law relating to the underground\nfacilities safety training account from October 1, 2015 until October\n1, 2020.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Underground Facilities Safety Training Account (UFSTA) and\none-call notification system established by the State in 2000 has made\nsignificant progress in reducing the number of accidents involving\nunderground utility facilities by providing contractors and others who\nhave occasion to do underground excavation with a single entity to\nwhich they can go to ascertain the location of all underground\nfacilities in the area in which they plan to dig.  However, the great\nmajority of accidents which do occur with such facilities are still\ncaused by those excavators who are ignorant of, or choose not to avail\nthemselves of, the one-call system. The availability of funding\nthrough the UFSTA has been successful in providing training and\neducation to operators and excavators on the requirements and\nprocedures for the protection of underground facilities. The UFSTA\nreceives penalty revenues from excavators or operators found to have\nviolated provision of law related to the State's one-call notification\nsystem. Specifically, excavators are required to contact the one-call\nsystem prior to digging and operators of underground facilities\n(primarily utilities) are required to accurately mark the location of\ntheir respective facilities.  Revenues deposited into the account can\nbe appropriated for training and education on the requirements and\nprocedures for protection of underground facilities.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nChapter 522 of 2000, Chapter 707 of 2005, Chapter 372 of 2010. The\ncurrent law expires on October 1, 2015.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThis bill will reduce New York energy costs while having a very\nminimal impact on the state's general revenue.\n\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nSection 1 shall take effect thirty days after it shall have become a\nlaw and shall expire and be deemed repealed October 1, 2020.\n\n",
